    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **James Wood** stands as our premier offensive anchor with a 62.71 efficiency score. Despite facing a tough arm in Kyle Bradish, **Wood** benefits significantly from a favorable park factor (+16%) and a leadoff spot (+10%), offering the highest ceiling of the day, though we are monitoring a 30% rain risk. Following closely is **Max Muncy**, who draws a premium matchup against the vulnerable Aldegheri. **Muncy**\u2019s score of 69.72 is inflated by a massive +19% SP skill boost, making him our most efficient play against a weak starter. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** and **Brice Turang** provide reliable stability in the 60-point range; **Naylor** leverages a strong #3 order position, while **Turang** capitalizes on a high-leverage opportunity against Rolison to maintain his utility.\n\nOur toughest bench decision involved **Alex Freeland**, who posted a 47.17, hovering just outside the cutoff. While **Freeland** offered switch-hitting versatility, his projected #9 spot in the order acted as a drag on his efficiency, ultimately keeping him on the pine in favor of the higher-upside profiles like **Zach McKinstry** and **Jose Caballero**. We are remaining cautious with **Dalton Rushing**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Austin Riley**, as their scores remain contingent on \"Lineup Pending\" status; if these players shift downward in their respective orders, their efficiency scores will require re-evaluation. While we are holding firm with our current construction, we continue to stash **Trevor Story** and **Mickey Moniak** on the bench due to injury and rest, respectively, while monitoring the depth provided by **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Carson Williams**, **Cole Young**, and **Henry Bolte** for future rotation needs. **Corbin Carroll** and **Seiya Suzuki** face difficult matchups against Rasmussen and Woodruff, respectively; while their base projections are sound, the negative SP skill adjustments highlight the necessity of closely tracking their at-bats as we manage our game caps.",
